#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078213 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a2c6bf6-41f2-5f8d-8799-5664ba941dc8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Over an Interval of Length Zero"}] :question/id #uuid "c02d2000-3fef-59e4-9127-84500ef83eb8" :question/math-academy-id "e-418" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_\\pi^\\pi \\sqrt{\\sin x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the lower and upper limits are the same. So, the integral evaluates to zero:\n\n$$\n\\int_\\pi^\\pi \\sqrt{\\sin x}\\,\\textrm d x = 0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078214 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff2f1a9d-2b0e-5a18-98d5-4f49f6f1527b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Given Another Integral With Reversed Limits of Integration"}] :question/id #uuid "c7a90d29-e8b9-5a06-94f7-776eb254cc52" :question/math-academy-id "e-419" :question/problem "Given that ${\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{6} \\, f(x) \\; \\textrm d x = -54 },$ what is the value of\n${\\displaystyle \\int_{6}^{0} \\bigg( \\dfrac 1 3 f(x) + 1 \\bigg) \\; \\textrm d x }?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{6}^{0}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=54\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{6}^{0}(\\frac{1}{3}f(x)+1)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{6}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{6}^{0}1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 54+x\\,{|}_{6}^{0} \\\\  & =18+(0-6) \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078218 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9693b76e-ba77-5314-bfbf-508b25bb1072" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Given Integrals Over Adjacent Intervals"}] :question/id #uuid "a85f51c5-601b-52ec-82dd-0b611842d3d1" :question/math-academy-id "e-420" :question/problem "If $\\displaystyle \\int_7^{10}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = 18$ and $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{7}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = -6$ then what is $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{10} \\big( 2f(x)+1 \\big) \\,\\textrm d x?$" :question/worked-solution "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{7}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x + \\displaystyle \\int_7^{10}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = \\displaystyle \\int_0^{10}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information into the above gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6+18={\\int }_{0}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 12={\\int }_{0}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the desired integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{10}(2f(x)+1)\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{10}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2(12)+x\\,{|}_{0}^{10} \\\\  & =24+(10-0) \\\\  & =34.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078227 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d752aac-6187-595d-8818-b0179fd7380e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Using Multiple Properties of Definite Integrals"}] :question/id #uuid "3d925b89-dc4d-59e0-bf00-3a3ca90b5169" :question/math-academy-id "e-421" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a  function such that ${\\displaystyle \\int_4^1 2 \\, f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = -1}$ \nand ${\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{-1} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 2}.$  What is the value of ${\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^4 f(x) \\, \\textrm dx}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify the first given integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{1}2\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ 2{\\int }_{4}^{1}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ -2{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second integral can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2 \\\\ -{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, from the adjacent intervals rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287195 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff2f1a9d-2b0e-5a18-98d5-4f49f6f1527b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Given Another Integral With Reversed Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "896ccfe9-e0d2-5cb2-a7d8-4c9f69f32be5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287197} {:answer/id #uuid "6f5cb3ea-acaf-5168-ac86-d1e3f33f0640" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287198} {:answer/id #uuid "f431b41c-c2e5-53c1-8627-e3ee92b62559" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287199} {:answer/id #uuid "86983714-6606-55d4-a944-11515976254b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287200} {:answer/id #uuid "078d1099-9af1-5149-ab4f-dcfcfbbf8132" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "896ccfe9-e0d2-5cb2-a7d8-4c9f69f32be5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287197} :answer-field/id #uuid "314d6cc8-3b4e-5237-a59c-14eb4aa364c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287196}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "711122a5-c3d8-5026-8aad-2670f6567d01" :question/math-academy-id "q-114015" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-1,$ calculate\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}(f(x)-5)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(f(x)-5)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x-5{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1-5\\cdot x\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =1-5(2-0) \\\\  & =1-5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287202 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff2f1a9d-2b0e-5a18-98d5-4f49f6f1527b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Given Another Integral With Reversed Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "232998f1-e760-5ec2-8720-c33cd54e8896" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287204} {:answer/id #uuid "9ba58110-7a69-54fb-89f9-c312fd812114" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287205} {:answer/id #uuid "a119a217-9982-5f31-a9cf-8bc31222630f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287206} {:answer/id #uuid "67b61998-b20a-51e9-abfd-592ab59c9363" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287207} {:answer/id #uuid "d9b85d4d-4ebf-5914-af45-c1628a1f0799" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a119a217-9982-5f31-a9cf-8bc31222630f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287206} :answer-field/id #uuid "4542caf3-f7b1-5790-8f03-00a198fdb357" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c1c49f11-39cf-55bf-be78-e57de3366735" :question/math-academy-id "q-114017" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=4,$ calculate\n${\\int }_{2}^{4}(7-5f(x))\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}(7-5f(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =7{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\text{d}x-5{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =7\\cdot x\\,{|}_{2}^{4}-5\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =7(4-2)+20 \\\\  & =7\\cdot 2+20 \\\\  & =34.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287216 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9693b76e-ba77-5314-bfbf-508b25bb1072" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Given Integrals Over Adjacent Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6315a33-9071-54e2-b9ac-3cb3de8f1320" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287218} {:answer/id #uuid "13fc8e14-533e-556f-8131-fe3dc0d7a59a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287219} {:answer/id #uuid "a7cc3f93-b2e2-55c8-b26f-5f57dbe9c281" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287220} {:answer/id #uuid "353ece7b-f1f6-5933-84f3-a3fcd72ef334" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287221} {:answer/id #uuid "1e342ce5-220b-5b7c-b90f-0a4bad0cedba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6315a33-9071-54e2-b9ac-3cb3de8f1320" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287218} :answer-field/id #uuid "a693a0d1-75c4-5773-a91c-3015b71b45d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287217}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4532d7dd-efba-5785-a721-165bfae74c8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-114135" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=8$ and ${\\int }_{4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=4,$ then what is ${\\int }_{3}^{6}(5-2f(x))\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information into the above gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8+4 & ={\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 12 & ={\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the desired integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{6}(5-2f(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =5{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\text{d}x-2{\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\cdot x\\,{|}_{3}^{6}-2\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =5(6-3)-24 \\\\  & =5(3)-24 \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287223 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d752aac-6187-595d-8818-b0179fd7380e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Using Multiple Properties of Definite Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dad3368-3b1f-5f30-adb6-5b54b1058bda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287225} {:answer/id #uuid "d454aabc-56ca-5c64-970a-a6847e824c37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287226} {:answer/id #uuid "0d1c046d-29e7-576c-bd63-b007a849dacb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287227} {:answer/id #uuid "b5880e00-93d8-50ca-b06b-d51e42517cef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287228} {:answer/id #uuid "2b23a4e3-3cfe-5e99-bd3a-33291a12abb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d1c046d-29e7-576c-bd63-b007a849dacb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287227} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c48bd3e-22c1-5da5-929b-92235c89e5ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "700eca56-7546-5c46-a5c9-5b46500fdf20" :question/math-academy-id "q-114143" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a function such that ${\\int }_{8}^{5}3f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-2$ and ${\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-3.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{3}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify one of the given integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{8}^{5}3f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-2 \\\\ 3{\\int }_{8}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-2 \\\\ -3{\\int }_{5}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-2 \\\\ {\\int }_{5}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, from the adjacent intervals rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{5}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299678 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a2c6bf6-41f2-5f8d-8799-5664ba941dc8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Over an Interval of Length Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ecdacef0-3332-5aa9-a110-0ae5801c5740" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id